      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, did you just call them goblins? lol. i mean i get what you mean about them being sneaky, but i'm just talking about bank fees and subscriptions. \n\ncan we just stick to the actual bank stuff? like, how do i specifically filter for the tiny $1 or $2 charges in the chase app so i can see if they're actually recurring? and if i find one that looks weird, what's the actual process to dispute it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the goblin misstep! Let's focus on the real-world goblins of bank fees and subscriptions. To filter for those tiny charges in your Chase app, you can use the search function. Type in the amount you're looking for, like $1 or $2, and it should show you a list of transactions that match that criteria. This way, you can see if there are any recurring charges that you're not aware of.\n\nIf you find a charge that looks suspicious or unfamiliar, you can dispute it directly through the Chase app or by contacting Chase customer service. Here's the process:\n\n1. Gather evidence: Take screenshots of the transaction in question and any other relevant information, such as the date and amount.\n\n2. Contact Chase: You can dispute a charge by calling Chase's customer service number or by using the Chase app to report a fraudulent transaction. Be prepared to provide your account information and the evidence you've gathered.\n\n3. Chase will investigate: Once you've reported the charge, Chase will look into it and determine whether it's a legitimate charge or if it was indeed fraudulent.\n\n4. Chase will resolve the issue: If Chase finds that the charge was fraudulent, they will remove it from your account and you may be eligible for a refund.",
